"Yes. If an EC2 instance created by Terraform is manually deleted from the AWS console, the Terraform state still has the old resource information. 
During the next terraform plan or terraform apply, Terraform refreshes the state, detects that the resource no longer exists in AWS, and plans to recreate 
it according to the configuration. After creation, Terraform updates the state file with the new resource ID."

2) If your CI/CD pipeline is successfully completing all stages up to the Trivy scan, but the deployment stage is not happening,

I would first check the Jenkins console logs to identify whether the deployment stage is failing or being skipped.

I would verify the Trivy scan result and exit code, because if Trivy is configured with --exit-code 1 for HIGH or CRITICAL 
vulnerabilities, Jenkins may stop the pipeline and not proceed to deployment.

I would also check the Jenkinsfile for any when conditions, branch restrictions, or dependencies between the Trivy and 
deployment stages. Then I would verify that the Docker image was successfully pushed to the registry and that the
deployment stage is using the correct image name and tag.

If the pipeline reaches the deployment stage but the deployment fails, I would check the Kubernetes/EKS deployment logs, 
pod status, image-pull errors, permissions, and service configuration. Based on the logs, I would fix the issue and rerun 
the pipeline.”

3)  What all steps do you perform before deploying an application to EKS/ECR?

“Before deploying an application to EKS, I first build and test the application. Then I create the Docker image and scan 
it for vulnerabilities using Trivy. If the scan passes, I authenticate with Amazon ECR and push the image with a proper
version tag. After that, I verify the image is available in ECR.

Before deployment to EKS, I make sure the EKS cluster is available and the required Kubernetes resources such as 
Namespace, ConfigMap, Secret, ServiceAccount, Deployment and Service are configured. I also verify IAM permissions
and networking/security requirements.

Then I update the Kubernetes Deployment with the ECR image URI and deploy it using kubectl apply. After deployment, 
I check the rollout status, pods, services, events and application logs to confirm that the application is running 
successfully.”
4)  If the application is successfully deployed in EKS but users are unable to access it
  If the pods are running but users cannot access the application, I don't immediately assume the application is healthy.
I troubleshoot layer by layer: first pod status and logs, then readiness probes, Service configuration and endpoints, 
then Ingress or Load Balancer, and finally AWS networking such as security groups, NACLs, subnets, route tables,
target-group health and DNS. I also test connectivity from inside the cluster to isolate whether it is an application
issue or an external connectivity issue
